# Git commits for significant changes

When implementing work in this repo, **create git commits** for meaningful deltas—do not leave large or important changes uncommitted at the end of a task unless the user explicitly wants no commit.

**Always commit (with a clear, scoped message):**

- **New features** (new behavior, data model, user-facing capability)
- **Large or visible UI changes** (layouts, new sections, restyled editors)
- **Bug fixes** (incorrect behavior, regressions, broken flows)

**Commit message style:** imperative subject line (`fix:`, `feat:`, `style:`, etc.), optional body if the change needs context.

**When to skip or batch:** tiny one-line typo fixes, purely exploratory edits, or when the user asks to hold commits. Otherwise, prefer **one logical commit per completed slice** of work rather than one giant unrelated commit.

After substantive edits, if the session is wrapping up and changes are uncommitted, **propose or run** `git add` + `git commit` as part of finishing the task.
